A||N||4||Rick 88||To backdate or not to backdate? That is the question.||||5||
Z||000000||Rick 88||09-10-2001||07:05 PM||||When Pontiac did the recall on the V6 cars they disconnected the vent line that ran from the engine to the air filter housing. A new vent tube was run directly to the air intake tube. Would there be any disavantage to backdating to the original setup? Would the throttle body stay cleaner? What parts would I need to get? Just curious.<P>Rick <BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||5
Z||000001||JohnF||09-10-2001||07:13 PM||||Rick, the recall is very interesting.  It seemas as if GM was just trying to get the NTSB of their collective back.  So, they agreed to everything suggested.  Hey, the Fiero wasn't being manufactured, they had nothing to lose with regulating the Fiero owners thereby, making the NTSB politicians happy.  Who knows if GM went too far?  Probably so!  As another question, why was the drip rail removed from the front of the engine deck - cooling according to NTSB and the recall.  Obnoxious according to Fiero owners!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||09-10-2001||10:11 PM||||I have already reinstalled the decklid drip rail. I was thinking that the vent tube vapors would cause less build up on the throttle body by their original routing through the air filter. Any opinions on this?<BR>All I seem to be missing is the connector from the tube on the firewall to the rear valve cover. Anyone have a pic of these pieces?<P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierodave||09-11-2001||06:12 AM||||You don't want to do it unless you can get a better rubber seal for the valve cover!   on most of the Ca cars I got last year the recall work was not done and had the original  stuff (getting a 88 done I dropped at the dealer today) <P>I have seen people want to sell the fiero they had when a mechanic said they had water in the oil and it needed head gaskets  I drained the oil  flushed the crank case,  and fixed that grommet  and guess what  NO more head gasket problem LOL<P><BR>I found that on 80% of them the grommet on the valve cover had failed from engine movement becuase the hose was routed and clamped to the tube on the fire wall and would let water run right into the engines valve cover on the firewall side this is with or with out the recall seal   <P>The rubber hose from the valve cover to fire wall seems to get stiff with age so engine movement  would wear <BR>out the valve cover grommet, and the metal elbow would fall right out of the valve cover!  anything can fall right into the engine on that side  there is a baffle on that side in the hole  but it won't stop water and dust from getting in there  <BR>just my 2¢ worth    FD<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierodave (edited 09-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rick 88||09-11-2001||09:44 AM||||Thanks fierodave  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'm going to leave that part of the recall alone! I was concerned that crancase vapors would build up more quickly on the throttle body and intake. Now I understand that this tube is a fresh air intake for engine ventilation. On cars without the recall could this also be a source for a vaccume leak? Now if I can only find a new PCV valve cover grommet. WTF?<P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
